the army in Lebanon has cleared all the  major roads in the capital Beirut and  other cities as anti-government protests  died down in the wake of Prime Minister  Saad Hariri s-- resignation on Tuesday  the Army had asked demonstrators earlier  on Wednesday to clear all roadblocks to  ensure that life returns to normal the  country sought 12 days of protests over  a weakening economy and corruption but  so far there been no protests on  Wednesday Hariri has been asked to  become caretaker Prime Minister until  the new government can be put into place  that's got lived' out of Beirut al  Jazeera Stephanie Decker is outside the  Prime Minister's residence in the  Capitol steps so the president has asked  the cabinet to stay on in a caretaker  capacity what happens next  yeah this is a line of the Constitution  the important things the big question  marks still going forward is now we're  waiting for the president announced what  they call binding parliament party  consultation so the heads of the  political bloc's in Parliament will be  discussing with the president as to who  they will appoint as a potential new  prime minister this in accordance with  the laws here needs to be a Sunni then  if there is agreement on that person  that person is then tasked with forming  a government and then that needs to be  agreed upon by the Parliament I've moved  it on by the part of it so there's a lot  up in the air we know there's a lot of  back-and-forth and disagreements within  the political parties so how long are  they going to take are they going to  come up with a format that will be  acceptable to the people I think that  still remains a big question here the  people of course want what they call a  technocratic interim body to rule this  country until there are new elections  under a new non-sectarian electoral law  so these are ambitious demands when  we've been out on the streets today  we've watched the army dismantling the  roadblocks not all protesters were happy  with that some saying that this is  giving into the government before all  their demands have been met others  telling us that no we need to give them  a couple of days we need to give them  two three days to see what's going to  happen what's going to be implemented  this lessens the stress on the roads on  the people trying to move around this  country and then we will wait and see so  nothing has been sold yet for sure I  think the challenges start now there is  a political crisis in this country and  again we know from the past this country  has seen political deadlock without a  government without a prime minister for  a very long time so everything is up in  the air as to what shape any interim  body is going to look like and also how  long it's gonna take to agree on that  and then the big question will the  people be happy no protests so far today  than Stephin life beginning to get back  to normal what about the banks have they  reopened  yes we're seeing a slow trickle back to  normal from what's been pretty much a  paralysis of the last two weeks banks  are not open yet they have announced  that as of tomorrow they're going to  resume normal transactions but they're  not physically going to be open their  doors just yet  we are expecting a statement maybe in  the next couple of hours as to when that  may be universities are slowly saying  that they will be resuming classes  tomorrow including the American  University of Beirut they also have been  closed many professors taking to the  streets with their students in these  calls as I mentioned also the roads now  all open across the country so there is  a sense of somewhat normalcy when you  come to just moving around the city the  fact that there's now more confidence  trying to be instilled that they are  saying the banks are gonna reopen but as  I mentioned this doesn't really solve  the bigger political issues on the table  it doesn't resolve the bigger issues  that the people still demand are they  going to lose momentum certainly the  people we've been speaking to will tell  you know that roadblocks can resume if  they're not happy with what's happening  that things can go back to where they  were but again these are very  unpredictable times I don't think anyone  can really tell you what the next few  days are gonna look like or the next few  weeks are gonna look like we're just  gonna have to wait and see al Jazeera  Stephanie Decker reporting live there  from Beirut Steph many thanks
 

